 ## Shared principles
-Builds on [Learning and Training Methods](9068-learning-and-training-methods.md).
+All methods in this family rely on differentiable rendering to compute gradients from the rendered image to the Gaussian parameters (position, covariance, opacity, spherical harmonics) and/or network weights [G]. The rendering process remains differentiable with respect to the scene representation [G]. Loss functions typically combine photometric reconstruction error, such as $\mathcal{L}_{L1}$ or $\mathcal{L}_{MSE}$, with perceptual or structural metrics like $\mathcal{L}_{MS-SSIM}$ or $\mathcal{L}_{LPIPS}$ [G].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [InstantSplat](6136-instantsplat.md)
-InstantSplat is a pose-free, sparse-view 3D Gaussian Splatting method that eliminates the need for Structure-from-Motion pipelines by leveraging geometry priors from foundation models like MASt3R or VGGT [6136-instantsplat.md]. It enables training in seconds by directly initializing Gaussians from these priors, distinguishing itself from feed-forward methods which regress primitives directly from images, and from traditional 3DGS which requires dense pose estimation [6136-instantsplat.md]. Choose this method when rapid reconstruction from very sparse views (3–10 images) is required without iterative optimization.
+InstantSplat is a pose-free, sparse-view 3D Gaussian Splatting method that eliminates the need for Structure-from-Motion pipelines by leveraging geometry priors from foundation models like MASt3R or VGGT [InstantSplat](6136-instantsplat.md). It enables training in seconds by directly initializing Gaussians from these priors, distinguishing itself from feed-forward methods which regress primitives directly from images, and from traditional 3DGS which requires dense pose estimation [InstantSplat](6136-instantsplat.md). Choose this method when rapid reconstruction from very sparse views (3–10 images) is required without iterative optimization [InstantSplat](6136-instantsplat.md).
+
+```python
+def instant_splat(input_images, intrinsics):
+    depth_maps, relative_poses = extract_priors(input_images)
+    point_cloud = back_project_depth(depth_maps, intrinsics, relative_poses)
+    gaussians = initialize_gaussians(point_cloud)
+    for step in range(num_steps):
+        rendered_images = render(gaussians, relative_poses)
+        loss = compute_loss(rendered_images, input_images)
+        gaussians = optimize(gaussians, loss)
+    return gaussians
+
+def back_project_depth(depth_maps, intrinsics, relative_poses):
+    points = []
+    for depth, pose in zip(depth_maps, relative_poses):
+        for v in range(depth.shape[0]):
+            for u in range(depth.shape[1]):
+                z = depth[v, u]
+                x = (u - intrinsics.cx) * z / intrinsics.fx
+                y = (v - intrinsics.cy) * z / intrinsics.fy
+                points.append(pose @ np.array([x, y, z, 1]))
+    return np.array(points)
+```
 
 ### [AnySplat](6137-anysplat.md)
-AnySplat is a pose-free feed-forward network that reconstructs 3D Gaussian Splatting scenes from uncalibrated image collections without requiring camera pose annotations [6137-anysplat.md]. It predicts 3D Gaussian primitives and per-image intrinsics and extrinsics in a single forward pass [6137-anysplat.md]. Unlike sibling methods, it scales to casual dense or sparse multi-view captures, matches pose-aware baselines, and surpasses prior pose-free methods in zero-shot evaluations at real-time rendering latency [6137-anysplat.md]. Choose this method when handling arbitrary multi-view captures where pose data is unavailable and real-time inference is needed.
